.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn BN_generate_prime_ex2
generates a pseudo-random prime number of at least bit length
.Fa bits
using the BN_CTX provided in
.Fa ctx .
The value of
.Fa ctx
must not be NULL.
.Pp
The returned number is probably prime with a negligible error.
The maximum error rate is 2^-128. It's 2^-287 for a 512 bit prime,
2^-435 for a 1024 bit prime, 2^-648 for a 2048 bit prime, and lower than
2^-882 for primes larger than 2048 bit.
.Pp
If
.Fa add
is
.Dv NULL
the returned prime number will have exact bit length
.Fa bits
with the top most two bits set.
.Pp
If
.Fa ret
is not
.Dv NULL ,
it will be used to store the number.
.Pp
If
.Fa cb
is not
.Dv NULL ,
it is used as follows:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy BN_GENCB_call(cb, 0, i)
is called after generating the i-th potential prime number.
.It
While the number is being tested for primality,
.Sy BN_GENCB_call(cb, 1, j)
is called as described below.
.It
When a prime has been found,
.Sy BN_GENCB_call(cb, 2, i)
is called.
.It
The callers of
.Fn BN_generate_prime_ex
may call
.Sy BN_GENCB_call(cb, i, j)
with other values as described in their respective man pages; see
.Sx SEE ALSO .
.El
.Pp
The prime may have to fulfill additional requirements for use in
Diffie-Hellman key exchange:
.Pp
If
.Fa add
is not
.Dv NULL ,
the prime will fulfill the condition p %
.Fa add
==
.Fa rem
(p %
.Fa add
== 1 if
.Fa rem
==
.Dv NULL )
in order to suit a given generator.
.Pp
If
.Fa safe
is true, it will be a safe prime (i.e.
a prime p so that (p-1)/2 is also prime).
If
.Fa safe
is true, and
.Fa rem
==
.Dv NULL
the condition will be p %
.Fa add
== 3. It is recommended that
.Fa add
is a multiple of 4.
.Pp
The random generator must be seeded prior to calling
.Fn BN_generate_prime_ex .
If the automatic seeding or reseeding of the OpenSSL CSPRNG fails due to
external circumstances (see
.Xr RAND 7 Ns ),
the operation will fail.
The random number generator configured for the OSSL_LIB_CTX associated
with
.Fa ctx
will be used.
.Pp
.Fn BN_generate_prime_ex
is the same as
.Fn BN_generate_prime_ex2
except that no
.Fa ctx
parameter is passed.
In this case the random number generator associated with the default
OSSL_LIB_CTX will be used.
.Pp
.Fn BN_check_prime ,
.Fn BN_is_prime_ex ,
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest_ex ,
.Fn BN_is_prime
and
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest
test if the number
.Fa p
is prime.
The functions tests until one of the tests shows that
.Fa p
is composite, or all the tests passed.
If
.Fa p
passes all these tests, it is considered a probable prime.
.Pp
The test performed on
.Fa p
are trial division by a number of small primes and rounds of the
Miller-Rabin probabilistic primality test.
.Pp
The functions do at least 64 rounds of the Miller-Rabin test giving a
maximum false positive rate of 2^-128. If the size of
.Fa p
is more than 2048 bits, they do at least 128 rounds giving a maximum
false positive rate of 2^-256.
.Pp
If
.Fa nchecks
is larger than the minimum above (64 or 128),
.Fa nchecks
rounds of the Miller-Rabin test will be done.
.Pp
If
.Fa do_trial_division
set to
.Sy 0 ,
the trial division will be skipped.
.Fn BN_is_prime_ex
and
.Fn BN_is_prime
always skip the trial division.
.Pp
.Fn BN_is_prime_ex ,
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest_ex ,
.Fn BN_is_prime
and
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest
are deprecated.
.Pp
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest
and
.Fn BN_is_prime
behave just like
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest_ex
and
.Fn BN_is_prime_ex
respectively, but with the old style callback.
.Pp
.Fa ctx
is a preallocated
.Vt BN_CTX
(to save the overhead of allocating and freeing the structure in a
loop), or
.Dv NULL .
.Pp
If the trial division is done, and no divisors are found and
.Fa cb
is not
.Dv NULL ,
.Sy BN_GENCB_call(cb, 1, -1)
is called.
.Pp
After each round of the Miller-Rabin probabilistic primality test, if
.Fa cb
is not
.Dv NULL ,
.Sy BN_GENCB_call(cb, 1, j)
is called with
.Sy j
the iteration (j = 0, 1, ...).
.Pp
.Fn BN_GENCB_call
calls the callback function held in the
.Vt BN_GENCB
structure and passes the ints
.Fa a
and
.Fa b
as arguments.
There are two types of
.Vt BN_GENCB
structure that are supported: "new" style and "old" style.
New programs should prefer the "new" style, whilst the "old" style is
provided for backwards compatibility purposes.
.Pp
A
.Vt BN_GENCB
structure should be created through a call to
.Fn BN_GENCB_new ,
and freed through a call to
.Fn BN_GENCB_free .
If the argument is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
For "new" style callbacks a BN_GENCB structure should be initialised
with a call to
.Fn BN_GENCB_set ,
where
.Fa gencb
is a
.Sy BN_GENCB * ,
.Sy callback
is of type
.Sy int (*callback)(int, int, BN_GENCB *)
and
.Fa cb_arg
is a
.Sy void * .
"Old" style callbacks are the same except they are initialised with a
call to
.Fn BN_GENCB_set_old
and
.Sy callback
is of type
.Sy void (*callback)(int, int, void *) .
.Pp
A callback is invoked through a call to
.Sy BN_GENCB_call .
This will check the type of the callback and will invoke
.Sy callback(a, b, gencb)
for new style callbacks or
.Sy callback(a, b, cb_arg)
for old style.
.Pp
It is possible to obtain the argument associated with a BN_GENCB
structure (set via a call to BN_GENCB_set or BN_GENCB_set_old) using
BN_GENCB_get_arg.
.Pp
.Fn BN_generate_prime
(deprecated) works in the same way as
.Fn BN_generate_prime_ex
but expects an old-style callback function directly in the
.Sy callback
parameter, and an argument to pass to it in the
.Fa cb_arg .
.Fn BN_is_prime
and
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest
can similarly be compared to
.Fn BN_is_prime_ex
and
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest_ex ,
respectively.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn BN_generate_prime_ex
return 1 on success or 0 on error.
.Pp
.Fn BN_is_prime_ex ,
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest_ex ,
.Fn BN_is_prime ,
.Fn BN_is_prime_fasttest
and BN_check_prime return 0 if the number is composite, 1 if it is prime
with an error probability of less than
.Pf 0.25^ Fa nchecks ,
and -1 on error.
.Pp
.Fn BN_generate_prime
returns the prime number on success,
.Dv NULL
otherwise.
.Pp
BN_GENCB_new returns a pointer to a BN_GENCB structure on success, or
.Dv NULL
otherwise.
.Pp
BN_GENCB_get_arg returns the argument previously associated with a
BN_GENCB structure.
.Pp
Callback functions should return 1 on success or 0 on error.
.Pp
The error codes can be obtained by
.Xr ERR_get_error 3 .
.Sh REMOVED FUNCTIONALITY
As of OpenSSL 1.1.0 it is no longer possible to create a BN_GENCB
structure directly, as in:
.Bd -literal
 BN_GENCB callback;
.Ed
.Pp
Instead applications should create a BN_GENCB structure using
BN_GENCB_new:
.Bd -literal
 BN_GENCB *callback;
 callback = BN_GENCB_new();
 if (!callback)
     /* error */
 ...
 BN_GENCB_free(callback);
.Ed
